    "correctIndex": 0,
    "explanation": "1. CONCEPT:\nConvert the process equation $P T = \\text{const}$ to the standard polytropic form $P V^n = \\text{const}$ using the ideal gas equation $P V = R T$.\n\n2. GIVEN:\n$P T = \\text{const} \\implies P \\left(\\frac{P V}{R}\\right) = \\text{const} \\implies P^2 V = \\text{const} \\implies P V^{1/2} = \\text{const}$. Thus, $n = 1/2$. For rigid diatomic gas, $C_v = \\frac{5}{2} R$.\n\n3. FORMULA / PRINCIPLE:\nC = C_v + \\frac{R}{1 - n}\n\n4. STEP-BY-STEP SOLUTION:\n1. Express in terms of $P$ and $V$:\n   $P T = \\text{constant} \\implies P^2 V = \\text{constant} \\implies P V^{1/2} = \\text{constant}$.\n2. Therefore, polytropic index $n = 1/2$.\n3. Molar heat capacity formula: $C = C_v + \\frac{R}{1 - n}$.\n4. Substitute $C_v = \\frac{5}{2} R$ and $n = 1/2$:\n   $C = \\frac{5}{2} R + \\frac{R}{1 - 1/2} = \\frac{5}{2} R + 2 R = \\frac{9}{2} R$.\n\n5. FINAL ANSWER:\nOption A: $\\frac{9}{2} R$\n\n6. WHY OTHER OPTIONS ARE INCORRECT:\nOption B is $C_p = \\frac{7}{2} R$; Option C is $C_v$ of monoatomic gas; Option D is $\\frac{R}{1-n}$ alone.",
